/// A call matching no signature is an error, under both processors.
///
/// G declares one parameter of type Integer.  Called with a String, the
/// interpreter raises 'No matching signature for function.' and compiled code
/// RUNS IT ANYWAY, binding the String to the Integer parameter and printing
/// 'ran with x'.
///
/// ⚠️ A declared parameter type is a requirement, not a hint, and the compiled
/// half currently treats it as a hint.  This is a silent type hole today, with
/// no new feature involved -- and it has to be closed before overloading
/// reaches file scope, because a no-match is exactly what an unresolved
/// overload degrades into.
///
/// The test asserts the interpreter's answer, so it passes interpreted and
/// fails compiled -- the same shape as EnumMemberSymbol.
///
/// Fails with: compiled only -- the call succeeds where it should raise.
///
class Strict;
begin
    function G (A : Integer) : String;
    begin
        Exit 'ran with ' + Str (A);
    end
end

test 'A call matching no signature raises';
begin
    try
        WriteLn (Strict ().G (Str ('x')));
        Fail ('the call succeeded; it should have raised');
    except
        on E : String do AssertEqual ('No matching signature for function.', E);
    end
end
